#: README.md
msgid "LumenOSS Blueprint"
msgstr ""

#: README.md
msgid "A blueprint for an education management information system (EMIS). It describes the domain an EMIS records, the rules for handling its data, and the governed data platform that holds it. The architecture stays the same wherever you build it; only the implementation changes."
msgstr ""

#: README.md
msgid "It covers everything an EMIS holds, from learners, schools, teachers and staff to enrolment, attendance, assessment and the indicators reported from them. It follows that data through operational databases, a lakehouse, governance and access, reporting, backfill and migration, and it includes example products that use all of them."
msgstr ""

#: README.md
msgid "We describe each part of the architecture as a **capability**. Each capability has a **contract**, which is a list of numbered clauses that you can test and that name no vendor. An **implementation** connects each capability to real components and shows evidence for every clause. We plan two reference implementations: one entirely open source on Kubernetes, and one on Databricks."
msgstr ""

#: README.md
msgid "Status: blueprint 0.5.0, with 41 capabilities and 246 clauses. Contracts are drafted; no implementation has evidence yet."
msgstr ""

#: README.md
msgid "If you're new here, start with [getting started]{1}. It suggests an order to read things in, and a path for each role."
msgstr ""

#: README.md
msgid "How the blueprint changes"
msgstr ""

#: README.md
msgid "Anyone can propose a change, by writing a **LumenOSS Improvement Proposal (LIP)**. A LIP is a numbered document that moves through its stages in the open: Draft, then Review, then a 14-day Last Call, and then a decision. It works the same way as the IETF's RFCs, Python's PEPs, Bitcoin's BIPs, Ethereum's EIPs and Cardano's CIPs. Because the blueprint governs data about children, every proposal says what it does to learners' data. When a LIP is accepted, we write it up as a decision record and release it in a new catalogue version."
msgstr ""

#: README.md
msgid "[LIP-1]{1} describes the process, and you can start from the [template]{2}. Not every change needs a LIP, though. [CONTRIBUTING.md]{3} tells you which changes do and which are ordinary reviewed changes, how to set up, and what reviewers look for."
msgstr ""

